A||N||2||Call||Can You Do This With A Fiero?||||5||
Z||000000||Call||12-27-1999||03:14 AM||||I am going to be purchasing a Fiero soon and I was wondering, is it possible to make it a "Targa Top"? How much would it cost to do this and would any modifications to the frame have to be made? Thanks in advance.<P>-Call||||reg||5
Z||000001||TOM||12-27-1999||11:59 AM||||You would have to make rails kind of like subframe connectors. Contact someone that has done a convertable top and see what they did.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||12-27-1999||01:19 PM||||ASC (American Sunroof Corporation-I've lost their website) does targa tops.  They're expensive, but probably worth it as they are the #1 choice of most of the automakers.  One thing you might consider before taking the hacksaw to your roof--Where are you going to store your top?  There really isn't anyplace on the car big enough for it.  You could leave it at home, but then, when it starts to rain, you're kinda screwed.  You could try an accordian style like on some of the older 911 Porsches or the 308 Ferrari, but they leaked and you couldn't hear over the wind noise.  IMHO, you would be better off getting the T-tops.  At least you can store those while you're driving.  And I bet it would be cheaper, too.  There's one on eBay right now.  I've got a buddy that got a targa top on his Trans Am and it cost him better than $4000US.  Too expensive for me.||||reg||1
